@@s001 DFS: depth-first visit
@@s002 ora:node just visited,dato:on the stack (on the path),ok:finished,no:back edge (cycle)
@@s003 I start from <b>s</b>. I mark it as visited and go down to the first neighbour not yet seen: <b>a</b>.
@@s004 I go deeper and deeper: from a to <b>b</b>, from b to <b>c</b>, from c to <b>d</b>. Each node is "on the stack" as long as it still has neighbours to explore.
@@s005 From <b>d</b> the arc towards <b>a</b>: a is already visited and is not the parent of d. It is a <b>back edge</b>: it closes a cycle a–b–c–d–a.
@@s006 d has no other neighbours: it is <b>finished</b>. I go back to c, then b, then a: they too have finished. I return to <b>s</b>.
@@s007 From s the neighbour <b>e</b> remains, not seen: I go down there and finish. Each node is visited once, each arc looked at at most twice.
@@s008 dato:first component,ora:second component,ok:isolated
@@s009 Connected components: groups of nodes all reachable from each other. I launch a DFS from <b>a</b>: it finds a, b, c.
@@s010 All the nodes already visited are "settled". I choose a <b>not visited</b> node: d. New DFS: it finds d, e, f.
@@s011 The number of components is the number of times I launched the visit. Total: each node and arc once, <code>O(n + m)</code>.
@@s012 DFS: depth-first visit
@@s013 The DFS (depth-first search, visit in depth) goes deeper as far as it can; when it is blocked it goes back. It serves to find cycles, connected components and the order of the nodes that feeds the topological sort.
@@s014 The problem and the idea
@@s015 The problem: explore everything that can be reached, and remember <b>in which order</b> the nodes were visited.
@@s016 The idea: from the current node you go down to a neighbour not yet seen, and repeat. When a node has no more neighbours to visit it is <b>finished</b>, and the visit returns to the node from which it had come (the parent). The stack of recursive calls, or an explicit stack, keeps track of the current path.
@@s017 DFS from s: you go deep, then you go back
@@s018 Back edges and cycles
@@s019 An arc from a node <code>u</code> to a node <code>v</code> already visited, which is not the parent of <code>u</code>, is called a <b>back edge</b>. In the undirected graph it means that a cycle exists.
@@s020 The cycle is rebuilt by climbing the parents from <code>u</code> up to <code>v</code>. This is the simplest way to find a cycle in a graph: a DFS, stopping at the first back edge.
@@s021 The parent does not count
@@s022 In the undirected graph the arc you have just travelled towards the parent is obviously already seen. It is not a cycle. Always check "seen and not the parent".
@@s023 Connected components
@@s024 A <b>connected component</b> is a group of nodes all reachable from each other. To count them you launch a visit from a non-visited node, marking everything you find; you repeat until no non-visited nodes remain. Each launch finds one component. Total cost: <code>O(n + m)</code>, because each node and arc is seen only once.
@@s025 Two components: two visits
@@s026 Cost and when to use it
@@s027 Cost <code>O(n + m)</code>. Use the DFS for: <b>cycles</b> (back edges), <b>connected components</b>, <b>topological order</b> (the finish order, read backwards, see the dedicated page), <b>reachability</b> when you do not need the distance.
@@s028 Do not use it for the minimum distance without weights: it finds a path, not the shortest one.
@@s029 The code, side by side
@@s030 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
@@s031 <a href="../codice/visita-dfs.html"><b>→ Java code for this topic</b></a>
@@s032 At the exam
@@s033 At the exam
@@s034 For <b>cycles</b> or <b>order of dependencies</b> (prerequisites, order of computation) the answer is DFS, or topological sort. Write: <i>"Run DFS from an arbitrary vertex. Every back edge found closes a cycle. Time O(n + m)."</i>
@@s035 For the <b>distance</b> it is not DFS: it is BFS (see the BFS page).
